Why this structure: Anchored rubrics matter because without them, LLM scores drift and cluster around 4. Requiring quoted evidence keeps the scores honest: a score the reviewer cannot justify with text is capped. The test plan turns the evaluation into something runnable, not just an opinion.

Technique: Zero-shot with explicit scoring anchors and an evidence requirement. Few-shot would help calibration, and one scored example per dimension would be a good addition in practice.

Failure modes prevented:

Generous scoring from impressions alone, blocked by the quoted-evidence cap.
Vague feedback. The fix must be a concrete rewrite.
Assessment that stops at reading the prompt. The test plan requires checking behavior under stress.
Score inflation for prompts that sound professional. The anchors describe observable features, not tone.